{"id": "20528994", "revid": "2308770", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki?curid=20528994", "title": "Jacques-Louis de Valon", "text": "Jacques Louis Valon, Marquis de Mimeure (19 November 1659, Dijon \u2013 3 March 1719) was a French soldier and poet.\nMenin to Louis, Dauphin of France (1661\u20131711), he entered on a military career and became lieutenant g\u00e9n\u00e9ral. Louis XIV of France promoted the territory of Mimeure to a marquisate around 1697 for him.\nHe wrote many verses which were not printed. Backed by Fran\u00e7ois Louis de Bourbon-Conti, Madame de Montespan and Nicolas Boileau, he was elected to seat 3 of the Acad\u00e9mie fran\u00e7aise on 2 December 1707. His acceptance speech is said to have been written by Antoine Houdar de la Motte. Valon's last known literary work is a 1715 ode in imitation of Horace. The rue de Mimeure in his birthplace is named after him"}
